% ============================================================================
% determiner-type-alias — for the demonstrative determiner, the alternate
% name the same already-quoted Grammarly sentence states, decoded from a
% span already sitting unused inside `determiner-type.adj`'s own header
% (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `determiner-type.adj`
% (`determiner_type(type, description)`, article/demonstrative_determiner/
% distributive_determiner → their own defining sentences): that table's
% own header already reproduces, verbatim, the Grammarly sentence for the
% demonstrative determiner in full --
%
%     "Demonstrative determiners, also known as demonstrative adjectives,
%      communicate the placement of a noun in space or time."
%
% -- but the table's `type, description` schema only had room for WHAT the
% demonstrative determiner does, not the parenthetical alias clause naming
% its ALTERNATE name. New `determiner_type_alias(type, alias)` table
% decodes that clause as its own row:
%
%   demonstrative_determiner → demonstrative_adjective
%     "Demonstrative determiners, also known as demonstrative adjectives,
%      ..."
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? determiner_type_alias(demonstrative_determiner, $Alias)  % demonstrative_adjective
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like the
% parent's own): the single `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `determiner_type_alias(type, alias)` carrying the citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query -- the engine returns the
% alias WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains
% on any determiner type that is not the one tabled here (it never invents
% an alias). The query also runs BACKWARD -- bind an alias and recall
% which determiner type it names:
%
%     ? determiner_type_alias($Type, demonstrative_adjective)  % demonstrative_determiner
%
% This is honestly a ONE-ROW table: of the three determiner types the
% parent table names, only the demonstrative_determiner's own sentence
% states an alternate name -- the article and distributive_determiner
% sentences each define their type without naming any alias. A recall on
% either of those two real, already-tabled types -- `article` (whose own
% sentence: "An article is a determiner that precedes a noun or noun
% phrase and identifies it as either specific or nonspecific.", no alias
% stated) -- correctly abstains rather than inventing an alias for it.
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME span already quoted
% inside `determiner-type.adj`'s own header (its truth table, "quote
% (verbatim)" column, demonstrative_determiner row), from the SAME
% Grammarly "What Are Determiners?" article that table already cites --
% no new WebFetch, only a different schema decoding a part of that
% already-verified quote. `trust consensus` -- the same tier the parent
% table already carries (Grammarly is a reputable secondary reference,
% not a primary standards body).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table determiner_type_alias {
    columns type, alias

    row (demonstrative_determiner, demonstrative_adjective)

    source "Demonstrative determiners, also known as demonstrative adjectives, communicate the placement of a noun in space or time."
    locator "https://www.grammarly.com/blog/grammar/determiners/"
    trust consensus
}
